<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.3.1: Finding Connections between Graphs and Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00405</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.3.1: Finding Connections between Graphs and Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bebc0fd9-0fc6-4130-ac51-8f1a239cfbfd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Use the following information to answer questions 1 – 8. Here are graphs of two equations in a system.
</para>
<para><quote>Determine if each of these systems could be represented by the graphs. Be prepared to explain how you know.</quote></para><!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise id="eebf952f-4032-4e59-9cec-bc7ced7ea1b0" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\( \begin{cases} x + 2y = 8 \\x = -5 \end{cases} \) </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No.</para><para>The correct answer is No.</para></solution></exercise></section><!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How do you know?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      No, it cannot be represented by the graph. The graph for \(x=−5\) would be on the left side of the vertical axis, which is not what is shown.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_2c74867e-69a9-444d-a4c6-e8b52bef40e4" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\( \begin{cases} y = -7x + 13 \\y = -1 \end{cases} \) </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No.</para><para>The correct answer is No.</para></solution></exercise></section><!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How do you know?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      No, it cannot be represented by the graph. The graph for
      \(y=−1\) would be a horizontal line, but no horizontal line is shown.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="ff1e2211-7397-429e-b55e-634c9a9f59c7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\( \begin{cases} 3x = 8\\3x + y = 15 \end{cases} \) </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Yes.</para><para>The correct answer is Yes.</para></solution></exercise></section><!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How do you know?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Yes. \(3x = 8\) can be rearranged into \(x=\frac83\). The graph for \(x=\frac83\) is a vertical line to the right of the vertical axis. \(3x + y = 15\) can be rewritten as \(y = -3x + 15\). Its graph has a negative slope and a positive \(y\)-value for the vertical intercept.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="fc091107-08da-4173-b043-be60729cd590" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\( \begin{cases} y = 2x - 7\\4 + y = 12 \end{cases} \)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No.</para><para>The correct answer is No.</para></solution></exercise></section><!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How do you know?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      No. The graph for \(y = 2x - 7\) would have a positive slope and a negative \(y\)-value for the vertical intercept, neither of which matches the given graphs.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
